\title{Proof Strategy Mining: The Primitive Divisor Schema\\
for Strong Divisibility Sequences}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 27, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The classical primitive-divisor theorems of Carmichael (for Fibonacci numbers) and
Zsygmondy (for the sequences $a^n - 1$) are usually presented as separate results
with separate proofs, one resting on the Fibonacci recurrence and the other on the
multiplicative order of $a$ modulo a prime. We \emph{mine} the common proof strategy
from these two arguments and isolate the single hypothesis on which it depends: the
strong divisibility identity $\gcd(u_m, u_n) = u_{\gcd(m,n)}$. We package the strategy
as a reusable, domain-agnostic schema over an abstract \emph{strong divisibility
sequence} and re-derive, from this one hypothesis alone, the entire structural theory
of primitive divisors: a meet law, rigidity of primitive indices, the \emph{pinning
law} that a primitive divisor appears exactly at the multiples of its index, a
\emph{join law} for simultaneous apparition governed by least common multiples, and
exact counting/density formulas. We then introduce the \emph{rank of apparition} as a
canonical construction of the primitive index from a prime alone, yielding the
self-contained \emph{strong primitive-divisor criterion}
$p \mid u_m \iff \rank(p) \mid m$. Finally we instantiate the schema to both the
Fibonacci sequence and the family $n \mapsto a^n - 1$, recovering the Fibonacci law of
apparition and the multiplicative-order law as two specializations of a single
abstract theorem. Every result has been formally verified. The contribution is
methodological as much as mathematical: a celebrated proof strategy is
reverse-engineered into a precise higher-order schema and reused across domains with
zero duplicated argument.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation: strategies as objects}
Mathematical proofs are routinely described by their \emph{strategy} --- ``a
descent,'' ``a counting argument,'' ``a primitive divisor argument'' --- yet these
strategies are rarely written down as precise, reusable objects. This paper is a case
study in the opposite discipline, which we call \emph{proof strategy mining}: take a
celebrated argument, identify the \emph{minimal} hypothesis that makes it run, and
repackage the entire chain of consequences as a schema parametrized over that
hypothesis. New instances then become corollaries, and structurally identical
theorems from different fields are revealed as one.

\subsection{The two source theorems}
Our source material is two classical primitive-divisor theorems. \emph{Carmichael's
theorem} states that the Fibonacci number $F_n$ has a primitive prime divisor (one
dividing no earlier term) for all $n$ outside a small finite set; its backbone is the
\emph{law of apparition}, $p \mid F_n \iff \rank(p) \mid n$. \emph{Zsygmondy's theorem}
gives the analogue for $u_n = a^n - 1$; its backbone is $p \mid a^n - 1 \iff
\ord_p(a) \mid n$. The two backbones are visibly the same statement.

\subsection{The mined hypothesis}
Auditing the Fibonacci argument reveals that it uses exactly one property of $F$,
namely $\gcd(F_m, F_n) = F_{\gcd(m,n)}$, and the sequence $a^n - 1$ satisfies the
identical identity $\gcd(a^m - 1, a^n - 1) = a^{\gcd(m,n)} - 1$. We abstract both into
the hypothesis of a strong divisibility sequence and develop the entire theory from it.

\section{Definitions}
Throughout, $u : \N \to \N$ and all divisibility is in $\N$.

\begin{definition}[Strong divisibility sequence]
The sequence $u$ is a \emph{strong divisibility sequence}, written $\IsStrongDivSeq(u)$,
if $u_{\gcd(m,n)} = \gcd(u_m, u_n)$ for all $m, n \in \N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Primitive divisor]
A number $p$ is a \emph{primitive divisor} of $u_n$, written $\IsPrimitive(u, p, n)$,
if $p \mid u_n$ and for all $k$ with $0 < k < n$ we have $p \nmid u_k$. We call $n$ a
\emph{primitive index} for $p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Apparition]
The number $p$ \emph{appears} in $u$, written $\Appears(u, p)$, if there exists $k$ with
$0 < k$ and $p \mid u_k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rank of apparition]
The \emph{rank of apparition} of $p$ in $u$ is
$\rank(u, p) = \inf\{\, k \in \N : 0 < k \ \wedge\ p \mid u_k \,\}$, with $\inf
\emptyset = 0$.
\end{definition}

\section{Elementary consequences of the strong-divisibility law}

\begin{lemma}[Strong implies weak; \texttt{IsStrongDivSeq.dvd\_of\_dvd}]
If $\IsStrongDivSeq(u)$ and $m \mid n$, then $u_m \mid u_n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $m \mid n$ we have $\gcd(m,n) = m$, so $u_m = u_{\gcd(m,n)} = \gcd(u_m, u_n) \mid
u_n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Meet law; \texttt{IsStrongDivSeq.dvd\_gcd\_index\_iff}]
If $\IsStrongDivSeq(u)$ then for every $d, m, n$,
$d \mid u_{\gcd(m,n)} \iff d \mid u_m \ \wedge\ d \mid u_n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Rewrite using $u_{\gcd(m,n)} = \gcd(u_m, u_n)$ and the universal property of $\gcd$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Degenerate index zero; \texttt{isPrimitive\_zero\_everything}]
If $u_0 = 0$ then every $p$ is a primitive divisor of $u_0$: $p \mid 0$ and the
minimality clause is vacuous.
\end{lemma}

This explains why the substantive statements require $0 < n$; index $0$ is universally
primitive when $u_0 = 0$, as for both Fibonacci ($F_0 = 0$) and $a^0 - 1 = 0$.

\section{Rigidity and the pinning law}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of primitive index; \texttt{isPrimitive\_unique}]
For any $u$, if $0 < m$, $0 < n$, and $p$ is a primitive divisor of both $u_m$ and
$u_n$, then $m = n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $m < n$, primitivity at $n$ forbids $p \mid u_m$, contradicting primitivity at $m$;
symmetrically. Only the definition of primitivity is used.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pinning law; \texttt{dvd\_iff\_index\_dvd\_of\_primitive}]
If $\IsStrongDivSeq(u)$, $0 < n$, and $p$ is a primitive divisor of $u_n$, then for all
$m$, $p \mid u_m \iff n \mid m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(\Leftarrow)$ If $n \mid m$ then $u_n \mid u_m$ and $p \mid u_n$, so $p \mid u_m$.
$(\Rightarrow)$ If $p \mid u_m$, the meet law gives $p \mid u_{\gcd(n,m)}$; since
$0 < \gcd(n,m) \le n$ and primitivity forbids divisibility below $n$, we get
$\gcd(n,m) = n$, i.e. $n \mid m$.
\end{proof}

\section{The join law and counting}

\begin{theorem}[Simultaneous apparition; \texttt{simultaneous\_apparition}]
If $\IsStrongDivSeq(u)$, $0 < a$, $0 < b$, $p$ is primitive for $u_a$ and $q$ for
$u_b$, then $(p \mid u_n) \wedge (q \mid u_n) \iff \lcm(a,b) \mid n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the pinning law to each conjunct, then $a \mid n \wedge b \mid n \iff \lcm(a,b)
\mid n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-family join; \texttt{simultaneous\_apparition\_finset}]
Let $S$ be finite with, for each $i \in S$, a primitive divisor $f_i$ of $u_{g_i}$ and
$0 < g_i$. Then $(\forall i \in S,\ f_i \mid u_n) \iff (\lcm_{i \in S} g_i) \mid n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $S$ with base case $\lcm \emptyset = 1 \mid n$, using the pinning law at each
step.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Apparition density; \texttt{apparition\_count}]
If $\IsStrongDivSeq(u)$, $0 < n$, and $p$ is primitive for $u_n$, then for all $N$,
$\#\{ e < N : p \mid u_{e+1} \} = \lfloor N/n \rfloor$; the density is $1/n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The pinning law rewrites $p \mid u_{e+1}$ as $n \mid (e+1)$; count multiples.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Joint density; \texttt{simultaneous\_apparition\_count}]
Under the hypotheses of the simultaneous apparition theorem, $\#\{ e < N : p \mid
u_{e+1} \wedge q \mid u_{e+1} \} = \lfloor N/\lcm(a,b) \rfloor$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The join law rewrites the joint predicate as $\lcm(a,b) \mid (e+1)$; count multiples.
\end{proof}

\section{The rank of apparition: a self-contained criterion}

\begin{lemma}[Rank membership and minimality; \texttt{rank\_mem}, \texttt{rank\_le}]
If $\Appears(u, p)$ then $0 < \rank(u,p)$ and $p \mid u_{\rank(u,p)}$. Moreover if
$0 < k$ and $p \mid u_k$ then $\rank(u,p) \le k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The appearance set is a nonempty set of naturals; its infimum is a member and a lower
bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rank is a primitive index; \texttt{rank\_primitive}]
If $\Appears(u, p)$ then $p$ is a primitive divisor of $u_{\rank(u,p)}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Membership gives divisibility at the rank; minimality forbids it below the rank.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness via rank; \texttt{isPrimitive\_iff\_eq\_rank}]
For $0 < n$, $\IsPrimitive(u, p, n) \iff n = \rank(u, p)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(\Leftarrow)$ is the previous theorem. $(\Rightarrow)$ primitivity makes $p$ appear,
so the rank is a primitive index; uniqueness forces $n = \rank$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strong primitive-divisor criterion; \texttt{dvd\_iff\_rank\_dvd}]
If $\IsStrongDivSeq(u)$ and $\Appears(u, p)$, then for all $m$,
$p \mid u_m \iff \rank(u,p) \mid m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The rank is a primitive index; apply the pinning law at $n = \rank(u,p)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Join law in ranks; \texttt{joint\_dvd\_iff\_lcm\_rank\_dvd}]
If $\IsStrongDivSeq(u)$, $\Appears(u, p)$, $\Appears(u, q)$, then
$(p \mid u_n) \wedge (q \mid u_n) \iff \lcm(\rank(u,p), \rank(u,q)) \mid n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Rewrite each conjunct via the criterion and apply the $\lcm$ characterization.
\end{proof}

\section{Instantiations}

\begin{proposition}[Fibonacci; \texttt{fib\_isStrongDivSeq}]
$\IsStrongDivSeq(F)$ for the Fibonacci sequence $F$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from $\gcd(F_m, F_n) = F_{\gcd(m,n)}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Mersenne-type; \texttt{mersenne\_isStrongDivSeq}]
For every base $a$, $\IsStrongDivSeq(n \mapsto a^n - 1)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from $\gcd(a^m - 1, a^n - 1) = a^{\gcd(m,n)} - 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Fibonacci law of apparition; \texttt{fib\_dvd\_iff\_rank\_dvd}]
If $p$ appears in $F$, then $p \mid F_m \iff \rank(F, p) \mid m$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Mersenne law of apparition; \texttt{mersenne\_dvd\_iff\_rank\_dvd}]
If $p$ appears in $n \mapsto a^n - 1$, then $p \mid a^m - 1 \iff \rank(\cdot, p) \mid m$;
for $\gcd(a,p)=1$ the rank is $\ord_p(a)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Worked numerical examples}
\textbf{Fibonacci, $p=11$.} $11 \mid F_{10} = 55$ and $11 \nmid F_k$ for $0<k<10$, so
$\rank(F, 11) = 10$ and $11 \mid F_m \iff 10 \mid m$; indeed $F_{20} = 6765 =
3\cdot 5\cdot 11\cdot 41$.
\textbf{Two primes.} $\rank(F,2)=3$, $\rank(F,11)=10$, so $2$ and $11$ co-divide $F_n$
iff $\lcm(3,10)=30 \mid n$.
\textbf{Mersenne, $a=2$, $p=7$.} $7 \mid 2^3-1=7$, $\rank=3=\ord_7(2)$, so $7 \mid 2^m-1
\iff 3 \mid m$.
\textbf{Density.} For $p=11$ in $F$ and $N=100$, exactly $\lfloor 100/10 \rfloor = 10$
indices are apparition indices, confirming density $1/10$.

\section{Discussion}
The development is an instance of a repeatable template: \emph{audit} a celebrated
proof to record which concrete properties each step consumes; \emph{isolate} the
minimal hypothesis; \emph{abstract} every consequence over that hypothesis; and
\emph{reinstantiate} by verifying the hypothesis for each concrete object. The audit
is where the mathematics lies: neither the Fibonacci recurrence nor Binet's formula is
ever used --- only the gcd identity.

The schema captures the structural core (the law of apparition and its consequences)
but deliberately not the existence content of Carmichael's and Zsygmondy's theorems.
Existence reduces, after the structural reduction, to a single growth estimate: the
primitive part of $u_n$ must exceed $1$. This is a size inequality, not a divisibility
fact, and no lattice abstraction can supply it; the schema makes precise \emph{where}
the irreducible analytic work must go.

\section{Future work}
(1) Universal existence via a single sequence-agnostic growth bound
$u_n > \prod_{d \mid n, d < n} u_d$. (2) A characterization of strong divisibility
sequences as those that are multiplicative on coprime indices and satisfy the weak
divisibility law. (3) Cross-instantiation transfer between Fibonacci and Mersenne
primitive divisors. (4) Application of the template to Lucas and elliptic divisibility
sequences, and to other proof strategies.

\section{Conclusion}
We mined the primitive-divisor proof strategy shared by Carmichael's and Zsygmondy's
theorems, isolated its single load-bearing hypothesis --- the strong divisibility
identity --- and rebuilt the structural theory of primitive divisors as a reusable
schema: meet law, rigidity, pinning law, join law, density formulas, and the
self-contained rank criterion. Two instantiations recover two classical laws of
apparition from one abstract theorem, demonstrating that proof strategies can be
treated as first-class mathematical objects, reverse-engineered and reused across
domains.

\end{document}

